Message-ID: <OFF4861221.89355D77-ON8025745F.0038336B-8025745F.00386966@hsbcib.com>

Simon,

You were right of course. Somehow the cast did not appear to fail though. I
changed my code to match your suggestion and now it's all working fine.

Thanks!

// Giuseppe

> If I do something like this:
>
>      jengine = .jengine(T)
>
>      # Set the Java output and Err stream
>      routput = .jnew("org/rosuda/JRI/RConsoleOutputStream", jengine,
> as.integer(0))
>      .jcall("java/lang/System",
>                  returnSig="V",
>                  "setOut", .jcast(routput, new.class =
> "java/io/PrintStream"))
>
>      rerr = .jnew("org/rosuda/JRI/RConsoleOutputStream", jengine,
> as.integer(1))
>      .jcall("java/lang/System",
>                  returnSig="V",
>                  "setErr", .jcast(rerr, new.class = "java/io/
> PrintStream"
> ))
>
>
> Anything writing on the error stream will crash the GUI.Things
> writing to
> the output stream will make it hang. This happens when I set up both
> streamso or either of them...
>

Well, RConsoleOutputStream is not a subclass of PrintStream so the
above won't work (the cast is invalid). As the documentation for
RConsoleOutputStream says, you should be using something more like

.jcall("java/lang/System","V","setOut",.jnew("java/io/
PrintStream",.jcast(routput,"java/io/OutputStream")))

Cheers,
Simon

PS: You're entering quite dangerous ground there, I hope you're using
JRI controlled R otherwise things are likely to blow up...


