Subject: Re: Question: How to convert Java objects into corresponding representation in R
In-Reply-To: <207591308.219441212963169494.JavaMail.osg@osgjas02.cns.ufl.edu>
References: <207591308.219441212963169494.JavaMail.osg@osgjas02.cns.ufl.edu>
Message-ID: <EA37A8E3-3D50-4E4C-A777-0B62C4892867@r-project.org>

Howard,

On Jun 8, 2008, at 6:12 PM, HU,ZHENGJUN wrote:

> I used the following R command to get the returned date.001:
> date.001 <- .jcall(staff.001, returnSig="Ljava/util/Date;",  
> "getHireDay",
> evalArray=TRUE, evalString=TRUE, check=TRUE, interface="RcallMethod")
>
> I run the following R command print(date.001), and got the result  
> below:
> [1] "Java-Object{Sun Oct 01 00:00:00 EDT 1989}"
>
> I run .jclass(date.001), and got:
> [1] "jobjRef"
> attr(,"package")
> [1] "rJava"
>
> My question is: Is it possible to convert the returned date.001 from  
> java or Java-Object{Sun Oct 01 00:00:00 EDT 1989} into the  
> corresponding representation in R, e.g. Date in R?


Yes, by converting it to epoch date using getTime method:

x = .jcall(date.001, "J", "getTime")/1000
class(x)=c("POSIXt","POSIXct")

For example:

 > a = .jnew("java/util/Date","2007/04/14 20:00:00")
 > a
[1] "Java-Object{Sat Apr 14 20:00:00 EDT 2007}"
 > x = .jcall(a, "J", "getTime")/1000
 > x
[1] 1176595200
 > class(x)=c("POSIXt","POSIXct")
 > x
[1] "2007-04-14 20:00:00 EDT"


I suggest this hack as opposed to as.POSIXct, because with as.POSIXct  
you get into trouble with time zones as it is trying to be too smart  
for its own good. A hack-free alternative is:

x = as.POSIXct(.jcall(date.001, "J", "getTime")/ 
1000,origin="1970-01-01")-as.double(as.POSIXct(0,origin="1970-01-01"))

Cheers,
Simon


